The [[Provinces and territories of Canada|Canadian province]] of [[Saskatchewan]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s in 1906. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|licence plate]]s for display until 1912, when the province began to issue plates.<ref>{{cite web |last=Tanner |first=Eric N. |title=Saskatchewan Pre-Provincial License Plates |url=https://allaboutlicenseplates.com/displayPage.asp?country=CANADA&p=PrestateState&jurisdiction=Saskatchewan&code=sk |publisher=allaboutlicenseplates.com |access-date=January 23, 2022 |archive-date=January 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220123185834/https://allaboutlicenseplates.com/displayPage.asp?country=CANADA&p=PrestateState&jurisdiction=Saskatchewan&code=sk |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{As of|2022}}, plates are issued by [[Saskatchewan Government Insurance]]. Only rear plates have been required since June 30, 2004.<ref name="singleplate">{{cite web |title=Saskatchewan moves to a single plate on June 30th |url=https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2004/june/28/saskatchewan-moves-to-a-single-plate-on-june-30th |publisher=Government of Saskatchewan |date=June 28, 2004 |access-date=March 22, 2020 |archive-date=March 22, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200322203930/https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2004/june/28/saskatchewan-moves-to-a-single-plate-on-june-30th |url-status=live }}</ref>

